The subtraction can overflow for valid int values, which is undefined behavior. Compare the values directly:

```c
static int compare_ints(const void *a, const void *b) {
    int x = *(const int *)a;
    int y = *(const int *)b;
    return (x > y) - (x < y);
}
```

The result is negative, zero or positive without subtracting the input values.

Check both encoding failure and truncation:

```c
int n = snprintf(label, sizeof label, "%s:%d", name, value);
if (n < 0) {
    /* formatting failed */
} else if ((size_t)n >= sizeof label) {
    /* output was truncated */
}
```

On success, snprintf returns the number of characters it would have written, excluding the terminator. Equality with the buffer size therefore means truncation too.

Check conversion, range and the entire remaining suffix:

```c
#include <errno.h>
#include <limits.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

static int parse_int(const char *s, int *out) {
    char *end;
    errno = 0;
    long n = strtol(s, &end, 10);
    if (end == s || *end != '\0' || errno == ERANGE ||
        n < INT_MIN || n > INT_MAX)
        return 0;
    *out = (int)n;
    return 1;
}
```

The output is assigned only on success. Both pointers must be valid; strtol handles the permitted leading whitespace.

Check the element count before multiplying:

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

static double *make_values(size_t count) {
    double *values;
    if (count == 0 || count > SIZE_MAX / sizeof *values)
        return NULL;
    values = malloc(count * sizeof *values);
    return values;
}
```

The division gives the largest safe element count. malloc already returns NULL if allocation fails; successful elements remain uninitialized.

Compare the lengths first, then the bytes:

```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

static int bytes_equal(const void *a, size_t na,
                       const void *b, size_t nb) {
    return na == nb && (na == 0 || memcmp(a, b, na) == 0);
}
```

Each nonempty buffer must point to at least its stated length. strcmp is unsuitable because embedded zero bytes terminate strings. This comparison is not intended to be constant-time.

forEach ignores the promises returned by its callback and returns undefined. Awaiting that return value does not wait for the saves. Use a sequential loop:

```js
for (const item of items) {
  await save(item);
}
```

Each save finishes before the next begins. A rejection stops the loop and propagates to the caller.

